# iter (step 7.10): Go's package, with the two runtime coroutine functions (runtime.newcoro and runtime.coroswitch, linked with
# //go:linkname) as natives with a uintptr handle. gocpp's goroutines are OS threads (ADR-007), so a coroutine is a goroutine
# that never runs at the same time as the goroutine that switched to it (runtime/include/gocpp/iter.hpp, runtime/src/iter.cpp).

@note The coroutine is a native (gp::iter::newcoro, gp::iter::coroswitch) over a goroutine of its own: Pull and Pull2 are Go's code. The handle is a uintptr instead of a *coro, so the natives need no generated type. runtime.NumGoroutine counts the coroutine from Pull until the sequence is over or stop returns, as in Go; a Pull whose sequence is neither finished nor stopped leaks its goroutine, as in Go. Every switch is a hand-over between two threads: about 0.15 to 0.7 microseconds when both threads are running on different CPUs (they spin for a moment before they sleep), about 1.5 microseconds when one has to be woken, 3 on a machine with one CPU, where Go's costs 30 nanoseconds. iter.Pull is for the occasional pull (zipping two sequences), not for a loop over millions of values: range over the function itself.

@file iter.go
@why The coroutine handle is a uintptr (a *coro of an empty struct would need a type the native C++ functions can name); newcoro and coroswitch are natives (gocpp:native through natives.txt, keys runtime.newcoro and runtime.coroswitch).
@replace
type coro struct{}

//go:linkname newcoro runtime.newcoro
func newcoro(func(*coro)) *coro

//go:linkname coroswitch runtime.coroswitch
func coroswitch(*coro)
@with
// newcoro and coroswitch are the runtime's coroutines (gocpp: natives, runtime/src/iter.cpp). A coroutine is
// named by a uintptr here, where Go has a *coro.

//go:linkname newcoro runtime.newcoro
func newcoro(func(c uintptr)) uintptr

//go:linkname coroswitch runtime.coroswitch
func coroswitch(c uintptr)
@end
@why The closure that runs on the coroutine gets the handle as a uintptr.
@replace count=2
	c := newcoro(func(c *coro) {
@with
	c := newcoro(func(c uintptr) {
@end
